<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tycho Brahe</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1546–1601</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tycho Brahe was a Danish nobleman and astronomer. His observation of a new star in 1572 challenged the belief that the heavens were unchangeable. From his observatory of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uraniborg">Uraniborg</a> on Ven, granted by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-ii">Frederick II</a>, he made the most precise naked-eye measurements of his time. In 1599 he became imperial mathematician to Rudolf II in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a>, where his data passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johannes-kepler">Johannes Kepler</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tycho-brahe.jpg" alt="Tycho Brahe" width="320" height="395" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portr%C3%A4tt_av_Tycho_Brahe_-_Skoklosters_slott_-_90153.tif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
